WEBVTT

1
00:00:07.408 --> 00:00:14.056
Mom popped her head into my room.

2
00:00:14.057 --> 00:00:17.203
"Your room is clean!"

3
00:00:17.204 --> 00:00:20.852
"I'm building my computer," I told her.

4
00:00:20.853 --> 00:00:24.147
"You have to build a computer in a clean space.

5
00:00:24.148 --> 00:00:28.530
No dust or cat hair."

6
00:00:28.531 --> 00:00:32.124
Dad came in and sat on my bed.

7
00:00:32.125 --> 00:00:37.304
He looked impressed when he saw the computer parts spread out on my desk.

8
00:00:37.305 --> 00:00:41.196
"So what do you do first?"

9
00:00:41.197 --> 00:00:44.034
"This is the power supply."

10
00:00:44.035 --> 00:00:47.772
I held up a black box with cords running out.

11
00:00:47.773 --> 00:00:53.184
"First I'll put it in the computer's case so I can plug the computer in."

12
00:00:53.185 --> 00:00:56.581
"Be careful!" said Mom.

13
00:00:56.582 --> 00:01:03.649
"I'll keep everything unplugged while I'm building," I said as I screwed the power supply into the case.

14
00:01:03.650 --> 00:01:10.375
"Next I have to work with the motherboard." I held up the board.

15
00:01:10.376 --> 00:01:17.541
Mom chuckled. "It looks more like a 'robot' board than a 'mother' board."

16
00:01:17.542 --> 00:01:22.221
"A motherboard is like the computer's nervous system.

17
00:01:22.222 --> 00:01:27.664
Other parts plug into it, and the motherboard lets the parts communicate.

18
00:01:27.665 --> 00:01:33.597
The motherboard makes sure the information in the computer gets to where it needs to go."

19
00:01:33.598 --> 00:01:41.751
"Your mother makes sure everyone in our family gets to where they need to go," said Dad.

20
00:01:41.752 --> 00:01:45.648
Mom laughed.

21
00:01:45.649 --> 00:01:49.010
I picked up a small square package.

22
00:01:49.011 --> 00:01:53.453
"This is the central processing unit, or CPU.

23
00:01:53.454 --> 00:01:57.335
The CPU does all of the computer's work.

24
00:01:57.336 --> 00:02:01.246
It's like the computer's brain."

25
00:02:01.247 --> 00:02:04.991
"That's a pretty thin brain!" said Dad.

26
00:02:04.992 --> 00:02:08.977
"How can it remember anything?"

27
00:02:08.978 --> 00:02:14.622
"The CPU isn't exactly like a person's brain," I explained.

28
00:02:14.623 --> 00:02:20.806
"It does the thinking for the computer, but it doesn't actually store information.

29
00:02:20.807 --> 00:02:24.485
There are two other pieces of hardware for that."

30
00:02:24.486 --> 00:02:27.670
I pointed to a metal box.

31
00:02:27.671 --> 00:02:32.166
"The hard drive is where the computer stores all of its information.

32
00:02:32.167 --> 00:02:37.543
When you save something, it's stored on the hard drive."

33
00:02:37.544 --> 00:02:41.112
Then I held up a rectangular piece.

34
00:02:41.113 --> 00:02:46.269
"This is RAM. That stands for random access memory.

35
00:02:46.270 --> 00:02:51.269
RAM is where the computer puts information while it's working on it.

36
00:02:51.270 --> 00:02:57.459
I'm putting in extra RAM so I can work on several things at the same time."

37
00:02:57.460 --> 00:03:03.375
"I could use extra memory!" Dad laughed.

38
00:03:03.376 --> 00:03:09.957
I attached the RAM and CPU to the motherboard.

39
00:03:09.958 --> 00:03:16.238
Then I carefully placed the motherboard in the case.

40
00:03:16.239 --> 00:03:20.194
"Is the computer ready?" asked Mom.

41
00:03:20.195 --> 00:03:26.779
"Not yet." I pointed out spots on the motherboard.

42
00:03:26.780 --> 00:03:32.085
"These places are where you add parts for sound and graphics.

43
00:03:32.086 --> 00:03:39.301
I got a good graphics card for playing games."

44
00:03:39.302 --> 00:03:46.566
I put the remaining parts into place and connected my monitor to the computer.

45
00:03:46.567 --> 00:03:51.832
"Let's plug it in now," I said, crossing my fingers for luck.

46
00:03:51.833 --> 00:03:56.724
Dad plugged the computer into a power strip, and I switched it on.

47
00:03:56.725 --> 00:04:01.953
We heard the computer start up, and then numbers scrolled up the screen.

48
00:04:01.954 --> 00:04:09.596
Mom and Dad cheered and I let out a sigh of relief.

49
00:04:09.597 --> 00:04:14.357
"Can you surf the internet now?" Mom asked.

50
00:04:14.358 --> 00:04:19.835
"Not yet. This is just the hardware," I said.

51
00:04:19.836 --> 00:04:27.121
"I still have to install software—the programs and code that tell the computer what to do."

52
00:04:27.122 --> 00:04:32.083
"Can you write code too?" asked Dad teasingly.

53
00:04:32.084 --> 00:04:41.286
I smiled. "That's my next project."
